      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bothell was incorporated in 1909 and grew from logging/farming to a bedroom community by mid‑20th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bothell%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Bothell’s name honors the Bothell family, whose first postmaster Gerhard Ericksen bought the boarding house that became the town’s namesake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bothell%2C_Washington",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A permanent campus shared with Cascadia Community College opened in 2000 on a site originally slated for a shopping mall, which was blocked to protect nearby wetlands.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bothell%2C_Washington",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Bothell Historical Museum sits in the Hannan House on Park at Bothell Landing and opens Sundays from April to October.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
